
Desktop tests for you and anyone maintaining this project
========================================================

These are repeatable examples with known answers. They run on the computer,
not in the robot's normal program. No ESP32, PWM or physical serial port is used.
They let you check a change before trying it with hardware.

In the VS Code terminal, with Locomotion Firmware open:

    .\test\run_desktop_tests.ps1

This builds and runs four C++ programs:

1. desktop/test_whole_leg.cpp
   Checks sampling and HOME/OTHER preview/accept using simulated ADC and serial.
   FAIL suggests a wiring expectation, sampling, capture or protocol regression.

2. desktop/test_installed_joint_configuration.cpp
   Checks the nine stored HOME/OTHER angle pairs, servo identities, q_0 and sigma.
   Passes the captured voltages through ServoCalibration and JointMapping, then
   checks the inverse conversion. Also checks that RR stays unconfigured.
   FAIL identifies a joint and whether data, direction, units or conversion differ.

3. desktop/test_measured_leg_pose.cpp
   Checks captured FR/FL/RL HOME feedback and an interior FR pose against known
   model angles and FK coordinates. Also checks simple FK axis/sign examples and
   that invalid feedback or unconfigured RR cannot create a new pose.
   The capture/link tests also check that POSE never modifies calibration records.

4. desktop/test_servo_command.cpp
   Checks q -> physical servo angle -> pulse width for every configured servo.
   Also checks asymmetric stop clearances, exact usable boundaries, invalid inputs
   and whole-leg rejection without partial output. The 2/3-degree clearances used
   here are test examples only, not physical robot settings.
   FAIL names the joint and whether mapping, table interpolation or rejection differs.

After building, run only the second program with:

    .\.pio\desktop_tests\installed_joint_configuration_test.exe

Run only the new ServoCommand test after building with:

    .\.pio\desktop_tests\servo_command_test.exe

Rerun the build script after editing code; the executable alone runs the last build.
If you intentionally replace calibration data later, review the test's expected
measurements too. Do not change an expectation just to hide a failure.

test_python_interface.py is the separate Python UI/client test. Run after the C++
script; it uses whole_leg_test.exe as fake firmware and never connects to a board.

test_feedback_visualization.py tests the new live view, plotting the real C++ FK
results from fake ADC values. It also checks stale/disconnected/invalid data and
leg selection. Run it with the quadruped environment activated for its plot libraries.

PASS means the software reproduces the supplied measurements and mathematical
expectations. It does not prove physical home alignment, feedback accuracy,
collision clearance or safe PWM limits. Those still need their physical checks.
